Guide_for_the_Perplexed,_Part_1.11.4.english:
Likewise, when God is placed in this relation [expressed by homonymy[^1]] with the *species* of beings subject to birth and destruction, it is also said of Him that *He is seated* (יושב); for these species are perpetual, well-ordered, and of a stable existence like that of the individuals of heaven. Thus, for example, it was said: *He that sitteth* (היושב) *above the circle of the earth* (Isaiah, 40, 22), which signifies He who is perpetual and stable, above the circuit of the earth, or its *turn*, in alluding to the things which are born there *in turn*[^2];

[^1]: That is to say, by the homonymy of which mention is made in this chapter.
[^2]: The author plays upon the double meaning of the Arabic word דור (as a substantive and as an adverb), and we have attempted to render this play on words by employing the word *turn*. Ibn Tibbon employed for the adverb בדור the word חלילה, taken in the sense which it has in the expression חוזר חלילה, and from this he formed, to render the substantive דור, the word חלילות. The obscurity of the two words employed by Ibn Tibbon has caused the copyists to frequently alter them, and the manuscripts, as well as the editions of the Hebrew version, present here many variations; one must read: אל סבוב הארץ ר״ל חלילותה רמוז לענינים ההוים בה חלילה. Al-’Harizi translates בדור אלארץׄ by בהקפת הגלגל, *by the revolution of the sphere*, which is a misinterpretation.
